No, it is indeed possible to create a workable Nethack scoring system.

The biggest problem, I think, is making the system so that it's both
accurate and reflects the game's history as a descendant of Rogue.
That implies that money and other valuable objects should comprise a
significant part of the game's final score--which is now true only if
the player dies very early.


> That and most of your list aren't really game goals.  The game's one
> defined goal is ascension, so you really at most need to measure:
>
> Got the Bell?
> Got the Book?
> Got the Candelabrum?
> Got the Amulet?

While I think xanthian was obviously on the wrong track, I think
that's very reductionist.  The point of score isn't to rate progress
in the game, it's to rate skill, and there is nothing wrong with
activities that raise score and nothing else.  And, remember that
Nethack is a game which is as often played to mess around in, as a
kind of "Dungeons and Dragons simulator," as played to win.

I think Nethack scoring should be based primarily on:
- Player wealth (major, most important because carrying lots of wealth
causes real problems for the player and has an arguable hard limit.
note the the gemstones, unique items, artifacts and amulet fall into
this category, and should each be worth at least as many points as the
same weight in gold)
- Player real experience (semi-major; it's easy for experience to head
into the stratosphere and overwhelm all other sources, and this is the
source of all farming activity I'm aware of)
- Miscellaneous sources.

Another problem with scoring is that farming works, because the game's
state can be brought to a place where arbitrary survival is possible.

And a final problem with scoring: with so many intelligent, motivated
players out looking for ways to game the system, no matter what system
they use, there will be people trying to game it immediately.
Designing a system which is resistant to that is very difficult in a
game as complex as Nethack.

> Dive for Victory obviously lets you dive deeper faster, but the Castle
